.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_READ 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_read \- read body bytes as they arrive (streaming handlers)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ssize_t chttpsvr_req_read(chttpsvr_req *req, void *buf, size_t buflen);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Reads up to buflen bytes of the request body into buf, batch by batch,
as they arrive on the connection.  The call reads the socket itself,
on the streaming thread that runs the handler; the reactor thread
never does this read.  It blocks that thread until at least one byte
is available, the body ends, an error occurs,
chttpsvr_config_t.stream_read_timeout_us expires with no new data,
chttpsvr_config_t.max_body_read_duration_us expires (when that field
is set), or the average rate of the body since the first call falls
below chttpsvr_config_t.min_transfer_rate_bps once
chttpsvr_config_t.min_transfer_rate_grace_us has passed.

Return values:
  > 0   The number of bytes copied into buf.
    0   The end of the body was reached, or buflen is 0.  A buflen of
        0 is valid, and the call then does nothing.
   -1   One of six things happened: req is NULL; buf is NULL while
        buflen is above 0; the handler was registered as a buffered
        handler with chttpsvr_register_handler instead of with
        chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler; the connection dropped
        or was reset; stream_read_timeout_us or
        max_body_read_duration_us expired, or the rate floor ended
        the read; or the body exceeded max_body_size mid-stream.
        Call chttpsvr_req_stream_error immediately after this call to
        tell the last three apart, since those are genuine,
        data-dependent transfer errors.  That function reports
        ccol_success for a NULL buf and for the wrong handler type,
        which you cannot tell apart from "no error"; both of those
        are static programming errors that happen on every call to
        the faulty code, not only sometimes.

When buflen is 0 the function returns 0 at once, whatever buf holds,
so a NULL buf with a buflen of 0 is valid: the call returns 0 and does
nothing, which matches the rules of POSIX read(2).  buf must not be
NULL only when buflen is above 0.

A call returns the body bytes that the connection has on hand, up to
buflen, and waits only when it has none.  It reads the socket at most
once to do so, and one read brings at most 8 KiB of body, so a call
can return far fewer bytes than buflen while more of the body is still
on its way.  As with read(2), only a return of 0 means that the body
has ended.

This function is meant for a streaming handler registered with
chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler; use chttpsvr_req_body for a
buffered handler.

The first call to this function for a request writes the interim
"100 Continue" response when two conditions both hold: the request
carried "Expect: 100-continue", and the request really has a body,
that is, a Content-Length or a chunked Transfer-Encoding.  That interim
response, which tells the client to go ahead and upload the body,
goes out before the function tries to read anything.  A streaming
handler can instead reject a request outright (for example for bad
authentication or an unacceptable Content-Type) by writing a final
response and never calling this function.  The server then skips the
interim response completely, so the client sees the real rejection
directly, exactly as when it never sends "Expect: 100-continue", and
the server never asks it to upload a body that the server was not
going to read.  A request that carries "Expect: 100-continue" but has
no body at all gets no interim response either, for the same reason:
there is nothing to invite the client to upload.

The trailer fields of a chunked request body (RFC 7230 4.1.2) are
parsed and checked as part of reaching the end of the body, and then
discarded.  They are not request headers, and chttpsvr_req_header
never returns one; see that page for the reason.
.fi
